    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d821">

                <pb facs="1112" n="1098" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg1098"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/15232: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-01-15T13:00:00+07:00">January 15, 1944—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 17—12:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>98. We are taking up vigorously with Chinese Government subject of our 97,
                    January 15, by formal note to Foreign Office and as soon <pb facs="1113"
                        n="1099" xml:id="pg1099"/> as it can be arranged personal interview with
                        Soong,<anchor n="5" xml:id="d821-f1"/> and Kunming [is] being instructed to
                    avoid publicity and report fully [and] promptly developments; to telegraph in
                    confidential code, repeating telegrams to Department.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d821-f1" n="5" xml:id="d821-f1-content">
                <p>T. V. Soong, Chinese Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
